A query is a box the store holds, found by an id and the key it was asked
with, and `remote.query` is an ordinary intrinsic an adapter calls — there is
no query construct and no mark. So what reaches the output is whatever the
inliner carried to the call site, which is where the box has to be keyed.

`&` is the caller's whole say in it. Here the url is passed as a cell, which
is a question that follows its source: the lookup moves to the read, and the
ordinary dependency walk re-evaluates it. So the query is not stored at all --
it is what every read of `res` becomes, and a re-evaluated lookup with a
different key starts a fetch. The value form, bound once in the constructor,
is in remote_http_fetch_snap.txtar.

Either way it is the same lookup under the id the adapter spelled, keyed by
the url, and inside the thunk the transport the language implements, down to
the Go package that performs the request.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `m\.res = remote\.Query` -- a box bound once never re-keys however often its url changes, so this form is stored nowhere
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `res ` -- the model carries no res field at all; there is nothing to hold between reads
deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `&m\.url` -- a pointer into a model the platform copies is not something a backend can be handed, so the cell reaches the key as a value
broken out/go/bubbletea -- a []string literal is passed where the helper takes []any, which Go will not convert
-- live.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import http "sngl:remote/http"

window(title="fetch") {
    var url = "https://example.com/"
    var res = http.fetch(&url)

    text(value=res.or(blank).body)
    button(text="go", @click { url = "https://other/" })
}

const blank = http.Result{status=0, body="", headers={}}

output {
    go {
        bubbletea()
    }
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
